# dispscan — shield defaults.
#
# Only *defaults* belong here (a plain `.conf` fragment always wins over a
# Kconfig.defconfig default). Hard requirements that must not be silently
# overridden live in dispscan.conf instead.

if SHIELD_DISPSCAN

# The scanner is a separate BLE device from the keyboard. Give it its own name
# so it can never be confused with (or shadowed by a stale bond of) the
# keyboard, whose name is set in config/charybdis.conf. Max 16 chars.
#
# This is now a genuine default rather than a fallback in a tug-of-war: since
# the shield was renamed to `dispscan`, config/charybdis.conf is no longer
# pulled into this build by prefix matching, so nothing else assigns this
# symbol. dispscan.conf still sets it explicitly — a .conf assignment is the
# unambiguous single source, and this default only covers someone building the
# shield without that fragment.
config ZMK_KEYBOARD_NAME
	default "CharyScan"

# The whole point of the device. nice_view.conf also sets this, but the scanner
# must not depend on being built together with a particular display shield.
config ZMK_DISPLAY
	default y

# The font selection USED TO LIVE HERE, as a `choice ZMK_LV_FONT_DEFAULT_SMALL`
# default. It has moved to dispscan.conf, for two reasons:
#
#   1. It is a hard requirement, not a preference. Every geometry constant in
#      custom_status_screen.c is derived from unscii_8's 8 px advance, so this
#      file's own rule at the top ("Only *defaults* belong here") puts it in the
#      .conf.
#   2. IT DID NOT WORK. Verified against a real build's build/zephyr/.config,
#      which showed CONFIG_ZMK_LV_FONT_DEFAULT_SMALL_MONTSERRAT_12=y and
#      "# CONFIG_ZMK_LV_FONT_DEFAULT_SMALL_UNSCII_8 is not set" -- i.e. the
#      opposite of what this block asked for. Kconfig resolves a choice using
#      the FIRST `default` it encounters in source order, and ZMK defines the
#      choice with `default ZMK_LV_FONT_DEFAULT_SMALL_MONTSERRAT_12` in
#      app/src/display/Kconfig, which app/Kconfig sources at line 284 -- long
#      before it sources shield Kconfig.defconfig files at line 721. A shield
#      default can never win that race. A `.conf` assignment can, because
#      .conf fragments are applied after the whole Kconfig tree is built.
#
# (The build still linked only because LVGL's own `default LV_FONT_DEFAULT_UNSCII_8
# if LV_CONF_MINIMAL` happened to pull lv_font_unscii_8 into the image via a
# different symbol. That is luck, not a guarantee -- see dispscan.conf.)

# --- Renderer limits ---------------------------------------------------------
# Unconditional: the renderer uses it in every configuration, observer or not.
# Mirrors the KEYBOARD's CONFIG_ZMK_BLE_PROFILE_COUNT, which -- like the central
# side above -- the wire does not carry. Slots above this render "BT?", so a
# keyboard built with more profiles looks like a decoder bug and is not one
# (plan doc, review finding #5, which flagged the old hardcoded 4).
config DISPSCAN_PROFILE_MAX
	int "Highest BLE profile slot the keyboard can report"
	default 4

# --- The BLE observer --------------------------------------------------------
# The product. Scans passively for the keyboard's status advertisement, decodes
# the 26-byte payload and drives the renderer from it.
#
# Declared HERE rather than in Kconfig.shield for the same reason
# DISPSCAN_FAKE_SOURCE is: it is shield-conditional *behaviour*, not the
# shield's existence, and ZMK's own nice_view shield declares its prompted
# NICE_VIEW_WIDGET_STATUS symbol in its Kconfig.defconfig too. The review's
# observation that Kconfig.defconfig is conventionally for defaults stands --
# but consistency with the symbol two blocks down (and with upstream) beats
# splitting two sibling symbols across two files.
config DISPSCAN_OBSERVER
	bool "Scan for the keyboard's status advertisement"
	default y
	help
	  Starts a 100%-duty passive BLE scan once ZMK's stack is up, matches the
	  4-byte magic FF FF AB CD in manufacturer data, decodes the payload and
	  feeds a liveness state machine that pushes AWAKE / DARK / NO SIGNAL to
	  the status screen.

	  Mutually exclusive with DISPSCAN_FAKE_SOURCE. With both n the renderer
	  still builds and honestly shows NO SIGNAL forever.

if DISPSCAN_OBSERVER

# Sequenced AFTER ZMK's own BLE init so bt_enable() has already run. ZMK uses
# CONFIG_ZMK_BLE_INIT_PRIORITY (default 50, zmk/app/Kconfig:507-509) at the
# APPLICATION level for zmk_ble_init (zmk/app/src/ble.c:834), and passes NULL to
# bt_enable(), which is Zephyr's synchronous form -- so a later priority in the
# same level is sufficient. +1 rather than a large offset so that raising ZMK's
# priority cannot silently overtake this. The code re-checks bt_is_ready()
# anyway; see the comment block in dispscan_observer.c.
config DISPSCAN_OBSERVER_INIT_PRIORITY
	int "dispscan observer init priority"
	default 51

# --- The battery L/R swap ----------------------------------------------------
# THE ONE PIECE OF THE CONTRACT THAT IS NOT ON THE WIRE. The payload carries the
# advertising device's own battery at offset 5 and the other half's at offset 12
# -- ROLE positions. Turning those into LEFT and RIGHT needs to know which
# physical half is the central, and NO FIELD IN THE PAYLOAD SAYS SO. It lives in
# the keyboard's CONFIG_ZMK_STATUS_ADV_CENTRAL_SIDE, in the other firmware
# image, and this symbol is the scanner's mirror of it.
#
# FLIPPING THE KEYBOARD'S CENTRAL TO THE LEFT HALF WITHOUT FLIPPING THIS SWAPS
# THE TWO BATTERIES ON SCREEN, SILENTLY. Both readings stay plausible, both keep
# moving, nothing logs. It is invisible until someone drains one half and
# watches the wrong bar fall.
#
# This is why the plan doc's D3 ("the only contract between them is the
# advertisement payload byte layout") is NOT STRICTLY TRUE. The contract is the
# byte layout plus the value of this symbol. Said plainly here rather than
# discovered later.
#
# Defaults to RIGHT because our Charybdis central is the right half (D7).
config DISPSCAN_CENTRAL_SIDE_RIGHT
	bool "The keyboard's central (advertising) half is the RIGHT one"
	default y
	help
	  Must match the keyboard's CONFIG_ZMK_STATUS_ADV_CENTRAL_SIDE. Set n if
	  the central is the left half. Verify on glass: unplug one half and check
	  that the bar which goes to N/A is the one you unplugged.

# --- D8 binding --------------------------------------------------------------
config DISPSCAN_KEYBOARD_ID_ALLOWLIST
	string "Comma-separated keyboard_id allowlist (empty = discovery mode)"
	default ""
	help
	  Values as PRINTED on the panel ("ID C0FFEE12"), e.g.
	  "0xC0FFEE12,0xDEADBEEF". Matching is strict equality -- no wildcards, no
	  promiscuous escape hatch, unlike upstream's channel matcher.

	  EMPTY IS DISCOVERY MODE: the display binds to the strongest keyboard it
	  hears and renders that keyboard's ID, so a freshly flashed device is
	  usable immediately. Read the 8 hex digits off the screen, paste them
	  here, rebuild. Setup and graceful degradation are the same mechanism.

	  Discovery mode will happily show a stranger's keyboard. That is the
	  documented cost of being usable out of the box; bind it once and the
	  only wrong output the device can ever produce is NO SIGNAL.

config DISPSCAN_DISCOVERY_RSSI_MARGIN_DB
	int "Discovery mode: dB a challenger must beat the incumbent by"
	default 6
	help
	  Only used when the allowlist is empty. A margin rather than a plain
	  comparison, so two similarly-placed keyboards do not trade the display
	  back and forth on measurement noise.

# --- Presentation ------------------------------------------------------------
# Band D's right-hand slot is one field with two possible identities, and this
# symbol is which. The keyboard id is an 11-character field that matters exactly
# once -- during binding, when the procedure is "read the hex digits off the
# panel and paste them into the allowlist above". After that it is a constant
# occupying a fifth of a 160x68 screen forever, so the slot switches to the
# packet RSSI, which answers the live question ("is this about to drop out")
# that the panel is otherwise silent about.
#
# DERIVED, NOT CHOSEN: it follows the allowlist being empty, i.e. exactly the
# condition dispscan_observer.c calls discovery mode. Deliberately a Kconfig
# symbol rather than a test in the C: the natural spelling there
# (`sizeof(CONFIG_..._ALLOWLIST) == 1`) is not legal in a preprocessor #if,
# and Kconfig compares strings natively. It also makes the coupling visible in
# menuconfig instead of buried in a header.
#
# No prompt -- overriding it independently of the allowlist would produce a
# display showing an id it is not binding on, or binding on an id it will not
# show. Neither is a state worth being able to reach.
config DISPSCAN_SHOW_KEYBOARD_ID
	bool
	default y if DISPSCAN_KEYBOARD_ID_ALLOWLIST = ""
config DISPSCAN_MAC_MODIFIERS
	bool "Draw Mac modifier keycaps instead of Windows ones"
	help
	  Picks which vendored glyph stands for the GUI and ALT classes: the
	  command and option keycaps when y, the Windows and Alt keycaps when n.

	  Cosmetic only. The wire carries modifier CLASSES, not key legends, and
	  cannot distinguish left from right at all (dispscan_status.h, trap #4),
	  so this changes what you see and nothing about what is decoded.

# --- Liveness / activity timing ----------------------------------------------
# Defaults from docs/remote-display-plan.md, D8 "Three liveness states".
config DISPSCAN_LIVE_MS
	int "Age below which a packet is LIVE (ms)"
	default 5000

config DISPSCAN_LOST_MS
	int "Silence after which the keyboard is declared gone (ms)"
	default 90000
	help
	  90 s = 3x the keyboard's ~30 s idle advertising interval, so a single
	  dropped advert can never trigger NO SIGNAL. It must stay well above
	  that idle cadence or a keyboard merely sitting there reads as dead.

config DISPSCAN_ACTIVE_GAP_MS
	int "Max inter-packet gap still counted as the ACTIVE cadence (ms)"
	default 3000
	help
	  The keyboard advertises at ~1 s while in use and ~30 s while idle, so a
	  gap under this means somebody is typing. 3 s = 3x the active interval,
	  tolerating two dropped adverts. Must stay far below the 30 s idle
	  cadence or the two are indistinguishable.

config DISPSCAN_DARK_MS
	int "Inferred keyboard inactivity before the panel goes dark (ms)"
	default 120000
	help
	  D6, honest-UX only -- with a USB-powered display this saves no
	  meaningful power. MUST exceed the keyboard's own ZMK_IDLE_TIMEOUT plus
	  margin (90 s default): go dark sooner and the panel blanks while the
	  keyboard is still advertising actively, then flickers back on the next
	  keypress.

config DISPSCAN_TICK_MS
	int "Free-running state re-evaluation period (ms)"
	default 1000
	help
	  DO NOT SET THIS TO 0 OR REMOVE THE TIMER IT DRIVES. The renderer seam is
	  push-only, so a dead keyboard produces no callbacks at all; without a
	  timer that runs when nothing happens, the panel holds a live-looking
	  screen forever. This is the single most important line in the observer
	  slice. Keep it well under DISPSCAN_LIVE_MS.

# --- Decoder self-test -------------------------------------------------------
config DISPSCAN_DECODE_SELFTEST
	bool "Run hand-built payloads through the decoder at boot and log results"
	default y
	help
	  NO BROADCASTER EXISTS YET (plan Phase 3 is gated behind an unstarted
	  soak), so the decoder cannot be exercised over the air. This feeds ~13
	  hand-built buffers through the real dispscan_packet_decode() three
	  seconds after boot -- past USB CDC enumeration -- and logs each decode:
	  the unmasked profile byte 0x10|3, a 4-character unterminated layer name,
	  a name truncated mid-codepoint, battery 0 meaning N/A, layer 255, all
	  modifier bits, an accepted minor bump, a rejected unknown major, a
	  25-byte payload, a zero-length payload, two wrong-magic payloads, and a
	  30-byte payload.

	  Set to n once a real broadcaster can do this job over the air.

# --- Packet trace ------------------------------------------------------------
config DISPSCAN_PACKET_TRACE
	bool "Log every accepted advertisement with its decoded fields"
	default y
	help
	  BRING-UP INSTRUMENT. Logs one LOG_INF line per accepted advertisement on
	  the BT RX thread, carrying the full decoded payload plus `dt`, the gap in
	  milliseconds since the previous accepted packet.

	  It exists because the panel cannot distinguish "the keyboard sent a value
	  we do not render" from "we rendered a value the keyboard did not send".
	  The first two defects found on air -- the left battery reading N/A, and an
	  apparent ~5 s refresh against a 1 s configured active period -- are both
	  decided by the raw decode and the arrival delta, so both are printed and
	  neither is inferred.

	  `dt` is what the RECEIVER observes, which is the honest number. It is not
	  the transmitter's configured period, and the two are allowed to disagree:
	  a missed advertising event shows up here as a doubled dt, which is exactly
	  the evidence needed to tell a slow transmitter from a lossy link.

	  SET THIS TO n BEFORE ANY POWER OR TIMING MEASUREMENT, and before the
	  device is considered shipped. It costs a log line per beacon and it keeps
	  CONFIG_ZMK_USB_LOGGING load-bearing.

endif # DISPSCAN_OBSERVER

# --- Development scaffold ----------------------------------------------------
# Drives the status screen from a synthetic status struct so the render path can
# be validated on hardware before any BLE code exists (docs/remote-display-plan.md,
# "Build order" step 2). This is the ONLY thing that calls
# dispscan_status_update() today.
#
# MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE WITH DISPSCAN_OBSERVER, and both are independently
# removable. `depends on !DISPSCAN_OBSERVER` makes the exclusion a Kconfig fact
# rather than a convention -- with the observer on, this symbol cannot be set at
# all and the file does not compile, so the two can never both be pushing into
# dispscan_status_update() and fighting over the panel.
#
# It is NOT deleted, on purpose. It remains the only way to exercise the
# renderer without a broadcaster, and NO BROADCASTER EXISTS YET (plan Phase 3 is
# gated behind an unstarted soak). Deleting it now would leave the render path
# with no exercise route at all until Phase 3 lands.
#
# `default y if !DISPSCAN_OBSERVER`: whenever the observer is off, something
# should still drive the screen, otherwise the device boots to a permanent NO
# SIGNAL and proves nothing. Turning BOTH off is legal and is a supported
# configuration -- the renderer builds and honestly shows NO SIGNAL forever.
# Nothing else depends on this symbol: CMakeLists.txt guards the source with
# zephyr_library_sources_ifdef, and the renderer holds no reference back.
#
# Declared here rather than in Kconfig.shield because it is shield-conditional
# behaviour, not the shield's existence. Same shape as nice_view's own
# NICE_VIEW_WIDGET_STATUS, which is likewise a prompted `config` inside that
# shield's Kconfig.defconfig.
config DISPSCAN_FAKE_SOURCE
	bool "Drive the dispscan status screen from a fake data source"
	depends on !DISPSCAN_OBSERVER
	default y if !DISPSCAN_OBSERVER
	help
	  Runs a slow Zephyr timer that synthesises decoded keyboard status and
	  pushes it to the renderer. The 40-step cycle sweeps each widget through
	  a representative range, visits all three display states (AWAKE / DARK /
	  NO SIGNAL), and then spends two dedicated ticks on the layout's actual
	  worst cases -- layer 255 with a full 4-character name, out-of-range
	  batteries, an illegal profile slot, WPM 255, every modifier held, and a
	  layer name containing a truncated multi-byte codepoint -- alongside the
	  wire contract's 0 = "battery N/A" case.

	  The panel carries a visible "FAKE" marker in every state while this is
	  enabled, so a device showing synthetic data cannot be mistaken for one
	  receiving real packets.

	  Development aid only. Set to n once a real BLE observer supplies
	  dispscan_status_update().

endif # SHIELD_DISPSCAN
